# The repository stores LF; the working tree gets LF too.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: without it, git's core.autocrlf decides, and the
# decision differs per machine. On a Windows clone with autocrlf=true every
# checkout rewrites LF as CRLF, and then EVERY file looks modified - the diff
# of a real change drowns in the diff of an invisible one. A review that
# cannot be read is not a review.
#
# "text=auto eol=lf" says two things: git NORMALIZES the line endings when
# it stores a file (so the repository only ever holds one spelling), and the
# working tree gets LF as well. The file's content decides whether it is
# text at all, so a binary file is still treated as binary.
#
# The two rules below are the exceptions, and they exist because those files
# are EXECUTED on Windows: a batch file with LF endings does not run.
# mvnw is the opposite case - a shell script that must keep LF, or it breaks
# on every platform.

* text=auto eol=lf

/mvnw text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
